  "text": "This brief report looks at a vocal game performed by a northern indigenous group, where the usual tools of music analysis—fixed notes and regular intervals—do not apply because the songs rarely use steady pitches or consistent spacing. The challenge for researchers is to capture the fine‑grained details of these performances, which involve two contrasting sound types: one produced with vibrating vocal cords and another made without that vibration. An early music theorist noted that the way people produce sounds can be as informative as the words they sing. Accordingly, the paper first sketches the overall shape of the pitch movements, then delves into a detailed comparison of the two sound categories, highlighting how each contributes to the overall musical expression."
